  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nSynoptically, the ridge remains to the south over the \nSouthern US / Gulf region. Guidance is in fairly good agreement \nregarding a shortwave moving through the region Friday through \nSaturday. However, some uncertainty remains as to how far south into \nthe Tri-State area it will extend. Sunday through Monday, flow will \nbe predominately zonal. Any shortwaves passing through the area will \nhelp increase precipitation chances. \n\nHigh temperatures for the week look to remain in the mid to upper \n80s for the majority of the Tri-State area, though both Friday and \nTuesday will be a tad bit \"cooler\" with highs in the 70s. Friday \nholds the best precipitation chances for the long term period, with \nthe Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) in the 20-60% range. The \nhighest amounts are expected to be the eastern portions of the Tri-\nState area, as the upper level shortwave moves through. For the \nremainder of the period. PoPs stay around 15-30% as various \nafternoon showers move through. Friday is currently forecast to be \nthe windiest day of the long term period, with sustained winds \naround 10-20 mph and gusts up to 35 mph. Areas most likely to \nexperience these winds will be east of the CO/KS border and south of \nI-70, primarily during the morning hours on Friday. \n\nTuesday into Wednesday next week look to be the most notable day of \nthe long term period, though it is about seven days out and a fair \namount can change. Currently, the Canadian-NH, ECMWF, and GFS models \nall show around a 571 dam upper-level trough moving through the Tri-\nState area, accompanied by a jet streak. The main uncertainty lies \nin how far south the trough tracks. It could pass as for south as \nthe CO/KS/OK border or as far north as the CO/NE border. \nAdditionally, all three deterministic models indicate some form of a \nsurface low on Tuesday, but with little agreement regarding its \nlocation. With all of that being said, this could be our next \nprecipitation system and will definitely be on our radar to keep and \neye on.",
